questions.stem:
A validation rule is placed so the failing case returns at once and only the surviving case runs the body. What does this program print?
static boolean eligible(int age, boolean member) {
    if (age < 18) {
        if (!member) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
System.out.println(eligible(30, false));
System.out.println(eligible(30, true));

A method answers the interesting case first and leaves a fallback for the rest. What does this program print?
static int discount(int total) {
    if (total > 100) {
        return 20;
    }
    return 0;
}
System.out.println(discount(150));
System.out.println(discount(40));

Two guards run in the order they are written, so the first one to fire decides the answer. What does this program print?
static String classify(String user, boolean active) {
    if (user == null) {
        return "anonymous";
    }
    if (!active) {
        return "dormant";
    }
    return "active";
}
System.out.println(classify(null, true));
System.out.println(classify("kim", false));

A guard inside a loop can skip the rest of the body without leaving the loop. What does this program print?
int kept = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) {
        continue;
    }
    kept++;
}
System.out.println("kept=" + kept);

A search that has found what it wanted can stop early instead of scanning the rest. What does this program print?
int[] data = { 4, 9, 16, 25 };
int found = -1;
for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
    if (data[i] > 10) {
        found = data[i];
        break;
    }
}
System.out.println("found=" + found);

A branch that returns makes whatever sits after it unreachable, and the other branch is still reachable. What does this program print?
static int classify(int score) {
    if (score > 0) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return -1;
    }
}
System.out.println(classify(5));
System.out.println(classify(-2));

A statement is left in a method after a return that always precedes it, and the compiler objects. What is the actual reason?
static int first(int[] values) {
    if (values.length > 0) {
        return values[0];
    }
    return -1;
    System.out.println("never");
}
System.out.println(first(new int[] { 7 }));

A method can finish without producing a value on one path, and the compiler refuses the file. What is the actual reason?
static int grade(int score) {
    if (score >= 50) {
        return 1;
    }
}
System.out.println(grade(10));

A method that returns nothing uses a different form of return to leave early. What does this program print?
static void log(String text) {
    if (text.isEmpty()) {
        return;
    }
    System.out.println("len=" + text.length());
}
log("");
log("abc");

A local is given a value in each branch and then used. What does this program print?
int bonus;
int score = 55;
if (score >= 50) {
    bonus = 10;
} else {
    bonus = 0;
}
System.out.println("bonus=" + bonus);

A method is written so that every path returns, and the two paths happen to agree. What does this program print?
static String status(boolean ready) {
    if (ready) {
        return "ok";
    } else {
        return "ok";
    }
}
System.out.println(status(false));
System.out.println(status(true));

A method rejects its input in two different ways and never returns a value at all. What does this program print?
static int parse(String text) {
    if (text.isEmpty()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("empty");
    } else {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("unsupported: " + text);
    }
}
try {
    parse("");
} catch (IllegalArgumentException problem) {
    System.out.println(problem.getMessage());
}

A block is left completely empty on purpose, and the code after it is ordinary. What does this program print?
int seen = 0;
if (seen > 0) {
}
seen = seen + 4;
System.out.println("seen=" + seen);

A test written with a literal always takes the same branch, whatever the data says. What does this program print?
int mode = 2;
if (true) {
    mode = 7;
}
System.out.println("mode=" + mode);

A test written with a literal never takes its then branch, so the other one is the only live path. What does this program print?
int mode = 2;
if (false) {
    mode = 7;
} else {
    mode = 9;
}
System.out.println("mode=" + mode);

A two way decision is written as an expression, so it produces a value rather than choosing a block. What does this program print?
int stock = 4;
String state = stock > 0 ? "in stock" : "out of stock";
System.out.println(state);

Several constants may share a single rule, which is how a group of cases is written in the arrow form. What does this program print?
char c = 'e';
String name = switch (c) {
    case 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u' -> "vowel";
    default -> "consonant";
};
System.out.println(name);

A statement switch is allowed to leave a value untouched when nothing matches and no default exists. What does this program print?
int code = 4;
String label = "none";
switch (code) {
    case 1 -> label = "one";
    case 2 -> label = "two";
}
System.out.println(label);

Two independent tests both assign the same local, and the order they appear in decides the result. What does this program print?
int score = 85;
String band = "none";
if (score >= 60) {
    band = "pass";
}
if (score >= 80) {
    band = "distinction";
}
System.out.println(band);

A chain of alternatives is tested from the top, and the first one that passes ends the search. What does this program print?
int level = 5;
String access;
if (level >= 1) {
    access = "member";
} else if (level >= 10) {
    access = "admin";
} else {
    access = "guest";
}
System.out.println(access);

A reserved value marks an absent entry, and the guard keeps it out of the arithmetic. What does this program print?
int[] queue = { 3, 0, 4, 0, 5 };
int count = 0;
for (int item : queue) {
    if (item == 0) {
        continue;
    }
    count++;
}
System.out.println("count=" + count);

A single value is tested against the two ends of a range, and the operator on each end decides whether the boundary belongs. What does this program print?
int age = 18;
boolean allowed = age >= 18 && age <= 18;
System.out.println("allowed=" + allowed);

A disjunction of one pair of equalities and one band covers a set of days. What does this program print?
int day = 10;
boolean weekend = day == 6 || day == 7 || (day > 7 && day < 14);
System.out.println("weekend=" + weekend);

A range is described by a low and a high bound, and nothing stops a caller from passing them the wrong way round. What does this program print?
int low = 30;
int high = 10;
boolean any = low <= high;
System.out.println("any=" + any);

One floating point value behaves differently from every other, and the difference shows up in every comparison. What does this program print?
double reading = Double.NaN;
boolean bigger = reading > 0.0;
boolean same = reading == reading;
System.out.println(bigger + " " + same);

A sort has to decide where a not-a-number belongs even though ordinary comparisons cannot order it. What does this program print?
double[] values = { 2.5, Double.NaN, 1.0 };
java.util.Arrays.sort(values);
System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(values));

A helper reduces a number to its sign, which is useful when only the direction matters. What does this program print?
int value = -42;
System.out.println(Integer.signum(value) + " " + Integer.signum(0));

A tolerance test has to ignore which of the two values is larger. What does this program print?
int current = 12;
int target = 20;
boolean close = Math.abs(current - target) <= 3;
System.out.println("close=" + close);

A value is confined to a range by nesting the two helpers, and the order of the calls decides which bound is applied first. What does this program print?
int raw = 145;
int clamped = Math.min(100, Math.max(0, raw));
System.out.println("clamped=" + clamped);

A method refuses an impossible request by throwing rather than by returning a wrong answer. What happens when this program runs?
static int withdraw(int balance, int amount) {
    if (amount > balance) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("amount " + amount);
    }
    return balance - amount;
}
try {
    System.out.println(withdraw(50, 80));
} catch (IllegalArgumentException problem) {
    System.out.println(problem.getMessage());
}

A library check for a missing value can be given a message to report instead of a bare fault. What does this program print?
static int length(String text) {
    return java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(text, "text required").length();
}
try {
    System.out.println(length(null));
} catch (NullPointerException problem) {
    System.out.println(problem.getMessage());
}

A value is validated while the object is being built, so an invalid one never exists. What happens when this program runs?
static class Timer {
    private final int seconds;

    Timer(int seconds) {
        if (seconds < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("seconds " + seconds);
        }
        this.seconds = seconds;
    }

    int value() {
        return seconds;
    }
}
try {
    System.out.println(new Timer(-1).value());
} catch (IllegalArgumentException problem) {
    System.out.println(problem.getMessage());
}

A conditional expression is allowed to produce no object at all on one branch. What does this program print?
static String lookup(String key) {
    return "a".equals(key) ? "apple" : null;
}
System.out.println(lookup("b") == null);

A guard written as a disjunction leaves the method as soon as either problem is found. What does this program print?
static int count(String text) {
    if (text == null || text.isEmpty()) {
        return 0;
    }
    return text.length();
}
System.out.println(count(""));
System.out.println(count("quiz"));

A guard written as a conjunction only allows the access when the object is there and the test passes. What does this program print?
static boolean openable(String state) {
    if (state != null && state.equalsIgnoreCase("open")) {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
System.out.println(openable("OPEN"));
System.out.println(openable(null));

A flag is initialised, set inside nested tests, and returned once at the end. What does this program print?
static boolean inRange(int value) {
    boolean ok = false;
    if (value > 0) {
        if (value < 10) {
            ok = true;
        }
    }
    return ok;
}
System.out.println(inRange(5));
System.out.println(inRange(50));

A running total would be wrong if a reserved entry were added to it, so a guard keeps those out. What does this program print?
int total = 0;
int[] data = { 5, 0, 7, 0, 3 };
for (int item : data) {
    if (item == 0) {
        continue;
    }
    total += item;
}
System.out.println("total=" + total);

A scan reports the first element past a threshold and then stops. What does this program print?
int target = 7;
int[] data = { 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 };
int position = -1;
for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
    if (data[i] > target) {
        position = i;
        break;
    }
}
System.out.println("position=" + position);

A ladder bounds the earlier bands and leaves the rest to a final open-ended branch. What does this program print?
int usage = 85;
String plan;
if (usage <= 50) {
    plan = "free";
} else if (usage <= 80) {
    plan = "basic";
} else {
    plan = "pro";
}
System.out.println(plan);

Two tests cover adjacent ranges, and the operator on the shared endpoint decides who owns it. What does this program print?
int page = 10;
String section = "other";
if (page <= 10) {
    section = "front";
}
if (page > 10) {
    section = "back";
}
System.out.println(section);

An assertion states a condition the programmer believes always holds, and it is not active in an ordinary run. What does this program print?
static int halve(int value) {
    assert value % 2 == 0 : "value must be even: " + value;
    return value / 2;
}
System.out.println(halve(10));

The same helper is called with a value that violates its stated condition, in an ordinary run. What does this program print?
static int halve(int value) {
    assert value % 2 == 0 : "value must be even";
    return value / 2;
}
System.out.println(halve(7));

An assertion message is an expression, and the language is careful about when it is worth building. What does this program print?
static String describe(int value) {
    assert value > 0 : "built:" + (value * 100);
    return "ok";
}
System.out.println(describe(1));

An index is validated before it is used, and the order of the two comparisons keeps the access safe. What does this program print?
static boolean check(int[] data, int index) {
    if (index < 0 || index >= data.length) {
        return false;
    }
    return data[index] > 0;
}
System.out.println(check(new int[] { 1, 2 }, 5));
System.out.println(check(new int[] { 1, 2 }, 1));

A ladder combines two independent facts, and a caller can match more than one rung. What does this program print?
boolean hasAccount = true;
boolean isActive = false;
String menu;
if (hasAccount && isActive) {
    menu = "dashboard";
} else if (hasAccount) {
    menu = "login";
} else {
    menu = "signup";
}
System.out.println(menu);

An enum with three constants is dispatched on by name, and every constant has a rule. What does this program print?
enum Status {
    NEW, RUNNING, DONE
}
Status status = Status.RUNNING;
String label = switch (status) {
    case NEW -> "queued";
    case RUNNING -> "active";
    case DONE -> "finished";
};
System.out.println(label);

A switch expression is used where a result is expected, so the rule values are what the method returns. What does this program print?
static String grade(int score) {
    return switch (score / 10) {
        case 10, 9 -> "A";
        case 8 -> "B";
        default -> "C";
    };
}
System.out.println(grade(92));
System.out.println(grade(75));

A switch expression is written where a value is expected, so it must produce one before the call can happen. What does this program print?
static int twice(int value) {
    return value * 2;
}
int code = 2;
System.out.println(twice(switch (code) {
    case 1 -> 10;
    case 2 -> 20;
    default -> 30;
}));

A switch expression is evaluated once per pass, and a block rule hands its value out with a yield. What does this program print?
StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    out.append(switch (i) {
        case 0 -> "z";
        case 1 -> "o";
        default -> {
            yield "n";
        }
    });
}
System.out.println(out);

Two behaviours are written as small objects, and a conditional chooses between them. What does this program print?
java.util.function.IntSupplier small = () -> 1;
java.util.function.IntSupplier large = () -> 100;
boolean big = true;
java.util.function.IntSupplier chosen = big ? large : small;
System.out.println(chosen.getAsInt());

An enum constant selects the rule named after it, and the two constants map to two different weights. What does this program print?
enum Mode {
    FAST, SLOW
}
Mode mode = Mode.SLOW;
int weight = switch (mode) {
    case FAST -> 1;
    case SLOW -> 10;
};
System.out.println("weight=" + weight);

A pair of comparisons is given a name, and the name is then used to make a further choice. What does this program print?
int hours = 12;
boolean workday = hours > 9 && hours < 17;
String shift = workday ? "day" : "night";
System.out.println(shift);

A named decision is inverted to ask the opposite question. What does this program print?
int score = 55;
boolean passed = score >= 60;
boolean failed = !passed;
System.out.println("failed=" + failed);

Two guards are written in a row, and the first one ends the method for the argument given. What does this program print?
static String tier(int age, boolean student) {
    if (age < 0) {
        return "invalid";
    }
    if (age < 18 && student) {
        return "young";
    }
    return "adult";
}
System.out.println(tier(-5, true));
System.out.println(tier(15, true));

The selector is itself an expression, so one decision feeds the next. What does this program print?
int hour = 20;
String bucket = switch (hour < 12 ? "am" : "pm") {
    case "am" -> "morning";
    case "pm" -> "evening";
    default -> "other";
};
System.out.println(bucket);

A text key that matches nothing is answered by the fallback rule rather than left without a value. What does this program print?
String code = "x";
int value = switch (code) {
    case "a" -> 1;
    case "b" -> 2;
    default -> 0;
};
System.out.println(value);

One rule of a switch carries a further decision inside its value, so two conditions have to hold. What does this program print?
int size = 3;
boolean gift = true;
String code = switch (size) {
    case 3 -> gift ? "G3" : "P3";
    default -> "PN";
};
System.out.println(code);

A ladder compares text values without regard to case, and the first rung whose test holds decides. What does this program print?
String kind = "PEN";
String label;
if (kind.equalsIgnoreCase("pen")) {
    label = "writing";
} else if (kind.equalsIgnoreCase("book")) {
    label = "reading";
} else {
    label = "other";
}
System.out.println(label);

A helper decides whether the value is acceptable, and only then is the value tidied up. What does this program print?
static boolean isBlank(String text) {
    return text == null || text.isBlank();
}

static String present(String text) {
    if (isBlank(text)) {
        return "(none)";
    }
    return text.trim();
}
System.out.println(present("  hi  "));
System.out.println(present("   "));

A key is made safe to switch on before the dispatch, so a missing value becomes one the rules can handle. What does this program print?
String suffix = null;
String label = switch (suffix == null ? "" : suffix) {
    case "" -> "none";
    case "s" -> "plural";
    default -> "unknown";
};
System.out.println(label);
